The mechanics, verified:

```ts
function format(id: string | number): string {
  // here: id is string | number — only shared ops are safe
  if (typeof id === 'string') {
    return id.toUpperCase();   // narrowed: string ✓
  }
  return id.toFixed(2);        // narrowed: number ✓
  // verified at runtime: format('abc') → ABC, format(3.14159) → 3.14
}
```

The narrowing toolkit (the interview answer):
```ts
// 1. typeof — primitives
if (typeof x === 'string') { /* x: string */ }

// 2. Truthiness — filters null/undefined out of unions
if (input) { /* input: string (from string | null) */ }

// 3. Equality — narrows both sides
if (x === 'error') { /* x: 'error' */ }

// 4. in — property presence narrows object unions
if ('send' in obj) { /* obj: has send */ }

// 5. instanceof — class narrowing
if (e instanceof TypeError) { /* e: TypeError */ }
```

The discriminated union (the pattern interviewers want):
```ts
type Shape =
  | { kind: 'circle'; r: number }
  | { kind: 'rect'; w: number; h: number };

function area(s: Shape) {
  switch (s.kind) {              // the tag narrows the WHOLE member
    case 'circle': return Math.PI * s.r ** 2;   // s: circle ✓
    case 'rect': return s.w * s.h;              // s: rect ✓
  }
}
// verified: circle r=2 → 12.6, rect 3×4 → 12
```

Why discriminants beat plain unions: without the tag, narrowing requires field-presence checks ('r' in s) — fragile when members share fields. The literal tag is a single, compiler-trusted switch.
